---
term: Absolute end
aka:
  - Absolute ends
  - Absolute purpose
section: absolute-ends
order: 10
definition: A purpose conceived in its fullest possible scope, including its conditions, sacrifices, opposition, and possible outcomes, that rests on irreducible principles.
seeAlso:
  - non-logical-axiom
  - protected-value
  - internal-justification
  - protective-belt
---
An absolute end isn't only a desired outcome. It's a complete conception of the reality being pursued. Nothing is left out because it's inconvenient to the pursuit. Even the suffering, resistance, autonomy, and competing purposes of the people affected belong in the conception of the end.

An end is absolute because its conception is complete and its foundational principles are irreducible. That doesn't require omniscience about every practical circumstance. Knowledge of the material world stays open to discovery, interpretation, and correction. So an absolute end can keep its foundational identity while its adherents keep learning about the circumstances of its realization. The distinction is between uncertainty about implementation and uncertainty about the purpose itself. See [[protective belt]].

Every absolute end rests on foundational principles that establish its identity and govern its realization. These principles are irreducible within the system that they constitute, and they take two forms: [[non-logical axiom|non-logical axioms]] and [[protected value|protected values]]. While those principles stay intact, the end's pursuit doesn't need external validation or universal approval.

An end can be absolute to its adherents without binding anyone else. Absoluteness isn't universality. Rawls's *comprehensive doctrines* take the same position (*Political Liberalism*, 1993): each one can be complete and binding for its adherents, while reasonable people still hold different ones. A comprehensive doctrine is a whole worldview, though, and an absolute end is one purpose with a complete account of its costs.
